WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:12.875
Hello. I'm Hank Green, the guy whose voice
you hear in a lot of Microcosmos videos.

00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:15.291
This is my face and this is my office.

00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:19.916
And I'm here to unbox the base
model of the Microcosmos Microscope.

00:00:19.916 --> 00:00:22.500
When I first started
getting interested in microscopy,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.666
it was very intimidating for me to try
and figure out what microscope to get.

00:00:26.666 --> 00:00:30.416
And so I'm really happy that we at Journey To The Microcosmos are able to provide

00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:34.041
the thing that we think is the best thing
to get you started.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.500
And I'm going to put it on the desk here.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.666
You'll see it. There it is.

00:00:38.666 --> 00:00:42.166
This is what it will arrive looking like.

00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:48.583
The first thing you will find
when you open it up is the manual.

00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:50.541
We built this manual up from scratch

00:00:50.541 --> 00:00:52.166
so you would understand
all the different parts

00:00:52.166 --> 00:00:56.250
of your microscope, how to use it,
doing your first samples, getting ready.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:00.458
There's also a QR code that links to
a bunch of video tutorials that we have.

00:01:00.791 --> 00:01:04.208
And this also is available
as a PDF and all updated versions

00:01:04.208 --> 00:01:07.416
will be available
at microcosmos.store and then

00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:10.458
there's

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.500
probably a bunch of ways to do this. But

00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:16.166
oh yeah, it's upside down now,

00:01:18.416 --> 00:01:20.500
then turn it right back right side up.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.750
Oh yeah, Look at that baby.

00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:25.958
“Enjoy the journey and share
what you find.” We do very much

00:01:25.958 --> 00:01:27.750
want to see your videos
that you take with this.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:33.208
I don't care if it's just some diatom
slowly gliding across the frame.

00:01:33.458 --> 00:01:35.041
I want to see what you find.

00:01:35.041 --> 00:01:36.083
We also have a submission

00:01:36.083 --> 00:01:38.416
form on the website
where you can actually submit your footage

00:01:38.416 --> 00:01:40.500
to potentially be used
in a Microcosmos video.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.041
We want to do like clips
of what y'all make.

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:47.250
Then this will slide off
if you give it enough reason to.

00:01:53.828 --> 00:01:59.606
So we have the head.

00:02:00.692 --> 00:02:03.208
Pull out the power cord here.

00:02:03.458 --> 00:02:07.083
There's this which is our phone mount,
which is a new version of the phone mount.

00:02:07.208 --> 00:02:11.000
A little bit of microscope oil
for the thousand times objective

00:02:11.625 --> 00:02:14.916
and then our microscope itself.

00:02:14.916 --> 00:02:16.625
This is just a blue light filter.

00:02:16.625 --> 00:02:18.750
Shoved in there will also be your cover.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.416
You want to cover your microscope
when you're not using it.

00:02:20.416 --> 00:02:22.416
So dust
doesn't get on the lenses and stuff.

00:02:25.235 --> 00:02:27.357
Hey! Hello!

00:02:28.088 --> 00:02:29.833
The best part of buying
something is taking off

00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:31.541
all the little things off of it.

00:02:31.541 --> 00:02:36.041
So you've got little tissue paper
covering up the light source down there.

00:02:36.125 --> 00:02:37.500
Little tissue paper

00:02:38.541 --> 00:02:42.500
in this space for safety

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.166
and then a little tissue paper on the...

00:02:45.166 --> 00:02:47.500
it’s the stage, is what that's called.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.708
Twist this little guy.

00:02:49.708 --> 00:02:50.958
Then you can pop this off.

00:02:50.958 --> 00:02:54.083
And then slide this in here

00:02:56.041 --> 00:03:00.166
and then twist it back in
so it doesn’t fall out.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:01.375
Doo doot.

00:03:01.375 --> 00:03:03.875
Take out these little guys, don’t
need those

00:03:04.708 --> 00:03:07.250
because your eyepieces go in there.

00:03:07.375 --> 00:03:08.250
Now remember

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.375
eyepieces are always loose, so
do not turn your microscope upside down.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:16.416
The eyepiece will come out and fall
onto the ground and little switch.

00:03:17.541 --> 00:03:19.541
Light is on.

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:24.500
I'm seeing a bunch of magnified
nothing now this is just magnified light.

00:03:24.916 --> 00:03:27.541
So there's a bunch of ways
to control your microscope.

00:03:27.541 --> 00:03:29.750
You've got the amount of light
that's coming out of the light source

00:03:30.083 --> 00:03:31.625
and that's adjustable here.

00:03:31.625 --> 00:03:33.000
These here are your focus knobs.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.750
The big one is, you know, big focus.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.083
The small one is more fine focus.

00:03:36.083 --> 00:03:38.125
There's this little thing
that actually collects the light

00:03:38.125 --> 00:03:41.291
and focuses it on to the slide
that's called the condenser.

00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:42.916
And you can move it up and down.

00:03:42.916 --> 00:03:45.000
And that affects how the light hits
the slide.

00:03:45.208 --> 00:03:48.416
And there's also a diaphragm
on the condenser, a little wiggly

00:03:49.375 --> 00:03:51.458
piece of metal that will bring the light

00:03:51.458 --> 00:03:54.958
sort of more into a point
which can affect how it looks.

00:03:55.166 --> 00:03:58.375
Basically, if you play around with it,
you'll start to see what I mean.

00:03:58.375 --> 00:04:01.333
And then on this side,
this actually moves the stage around

00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:04.416
so that you can look around the slide
or follow any critters

00:04:04.416 --> 00:04:05.875
that are running around.

00:04:05.875 --> 00:04:08.333
And that is the thing you'll get better
at fastest

00:04:08.458 --> 00:04:10.333
is what I've found is like
when I first started,

00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:12.791
I was like, everything's backwards
and it's so confusing.

00:04:13.125 --> 00:04:14.708
And then after
using it for a little while,

00:04:15.708 --> 00:04:18.416
I got very good
at it like not as good as James, but good.

00:04:18.416 --> 00:04:22.166
This is our phone mount that will come
with a separate little instruction manual.

00:04:22.166 --> 00:04:24.875
There's also instructions in the guide
for how to use the phone mount.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.125
Now, as you put your eyes on a microscope,
you'll see that there's a pretty small

00:04:29.125 --> 00:04:33.375
point of light as you approach it,
and it's easier with your own head

00:04:33.625 --> 00:04:37.000
to get your eyes in the exact same space
because you've been moving your head

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.333
around for your whole life.

00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:43.291
It's take some time after
you clamped this on here to get the it

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.000
right lens of your camera
focused on the right point of light.

00:04:47.375 --> 00:04:50.000
So it's a little fiddly,
but you fiddle around for a while

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:51.833
and then you're able to do it.

00:04:51.833 --> 00:04:53.500
And I actually find
that it can be more comfortable

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.291
to be using the phone mount

00:04:55.291 --> 00:04:56.333
for long periods of time,

00:04:56.333 --> 00:04:58.875
because I'm just looking at the screen
rather than sort of hunching

00:04:59.208 --> 00:05:00.250
into the microscope.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.250
So now we're going to use the microscope
as intended.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:04.875
I’m going to set up
my camera to be in here,

00:05:05.083 --> 00:05:08.125
and we have our prepared anatomy slides,
which are also available

00:05:08.125 --> 00:05:10.541
at microcosmos.store
and the box is so cool.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.000
So this goes on here, clamps on.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.791
So we're lining up the mount with right
kind of flush with the eyepiece.

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:20.208
You can see that right here, basically
on the same plane as the eyepiece.

00:05:20.333 --> 00:05:24.041
And then I'm going to get this boy
in here, which is going to take a while

00:05:24.041 --> 00:05:25.166
because I've got a big phone.

00:05:25.166 --> 00:05:26.666
Now with my phone,
because I've got a big phone.

00:05:26.666 --> 00:05:27.791
I have to clamp low down

00:05:27.791 --> 00:05:30.625
so that I can actually get the lens up
to where the hole is

00:05:31.125 --> 00:05:32.250
and slide that on there.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:35.916
Now, first I got to set to my
make sure I'm set to my double

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.416
lens, my two x,
I use ProCam to, to do this

00:05:40.416 --> 00:05:44.250
because otherwise Apple will mess with you
because it's trying to fix everything.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:45.958
All right, now I've got my

00:05:47.875 --> 00:05:50.208
sort of little circle right in the middle

00:05:50.208 --> 00:05:52.500
and I'm going to try and clamp it
there it is.

00:05:53.416 --> 00:05:54.583
Beautiful.

00:05:54.583 --> 00:05:56.250
And then I'm going to zoom in

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.708
and then I don't know what this is going
to be some loose connective tissue.

00:06:00.083 --> 00:06:02.375
My favorite kind of connective tissue.

00:06:02.375 --> 00:06:03.958
I'm seeing some pink.

00:06:03.958 --> 00:06:05.291
Let's try and focus.

00:06:05.291 --> 00:06:08.041
Focus, focus.

00:06:08.833 --> 00:06:10.875
A Oh, gross.

00:06:10.875 --> 00:06:13.708
This isn't this isn't the sexiest of

00:06:13.708 --> 00:06:15.250
objects to observe.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:17.333
Ciliated epithelium. Great.

00:06:17.333 --> 00:06:20.416
I actually do want to see some ciliated
epithelium now that I'm thinking about it.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:30.833
Some beautiful cells here to work with.

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:34.041
See all those nuclei in there?

00:06:34.041 --> 00:06:37.166
So this is all stained, so it looks extra
good.

00:06:37.625 --> 00:06:40.500
Now, we also have plan
objectives available.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.666
So slightly higher quality lenses

00:06:42.666 --> 00:06:45.791
which you can get a set of all four
to replace all the ones you have

00:06:45.791 --> 00:06:47.875
if you have the base model
and want to upgrade.

00:06:47.875 --> 00:06:51.833
We also have the 20 X
and the 60 X available separately

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.625
if you just want to get those one, one
or both of those on their own.

00:06:55.625 --> 00:06:57.541
I've just put in a lymph node.

00:06:57.541 --> 00:06:58.916
Oh yeah. Hello.

00:06:59.958 --> 00:07:01.458
My gosh.

00:07:01.458 --> 00:07:04.041
Boy, I tell you what,
I know nothing about histology.

00:07:04.041 --> 00:07:08.333
This is not my area of expertise now,
also on the bottom of our microscope,

00:07:08.333 --> 00:07:09.333
there is a filter tray.

00:07:09.333 --> 00:07:10.500
You can put that blue eyed filter

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:15.125
that comes with the microscope
or we have a set of various filters

00:07:15.125 --> 00:07:17.208
that do various things
and Rheinberg filters

00:07:17.208 --> 00:07:19.041
that will change the color of an image.

00:07:19.041 --> 00:07:19.916
So without the filter

00:07:19.916 --> 00:07:22.916
and then we're going to slide the filter
in place and see what happens.

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.125
You see there
you get like a dark field effect,

00:07:25.125 --> 00:07:27.958
which on anatomy slide
is not like the greatest thing

00:07:27.958 --> 00:07:30.416
because it's been dyed
for like the specific purpose, but

00:07:31.041 --> 00:07:35.416
with your microbes at your pond
locally looks fantastic.

00:07:35.458 --> 00:07:36.791
Now, the thing to note is
when we're saying

00:07:36.791 --> 00:07:39.750
like 40 X or 20 x or 60 x,
when it comes to the objectives,

00:07:39.875 --> 00:07:42.833
that's going to be multiplied by ten
because each one of these eyepieces

00:07:42.833 --> 00:07:47.708
also is a ten x,
So a 40 x is actually 400 times

00:07:47.708 --> 00:07:51.541
magnification because it's got
the 40 times the ten of the eyepieces.

00:07:51.541 --> 00:07:55.500
There's a fair bit to know
about microscopy, but I'll tell you what,

00:07:55.791 --> 00:08:00.416
as soon as you start to do it, it becomes
you have all of this incentive to learn

00:08:00.416 --> 00:08:03.833
and and then suddenly wake up one day
and you know,

00:08:04.375 --> 00:08:09.208
the difference
between a dinoflagellate and a rotifer,

00:08:09.291 --> 00:08:12.500
which if you watch this show,
you're probably already there.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:16.041
See, it's time to take the next step
and start on your own journey

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:18.833
into the microcosmos
with this beautiful object

00:08:18.833 --> 00:08:22.083
that I love having it in my office,
but this one's not for me, it's for you.

00:08:22.083 --> 00:08:23.541
We're going to pack it back up.

00:08:23.541 --> 00:08:26.375
I'm going to sign the instruction
manual for you.

00:08:26.458 --> 00:08:27.500
This is the base model.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:30.125
You can also get an upgraded version
that has the plan objectives,

00:08:30.125 --> 00:08:33.750
which are higher quality optics
already installed on the microscope.

00:08:34.083 --> 00:08:36.750
That is also available
at microcosmos.store.

00:08:36.833 --> 00:08:39.500
As I always say
at the end of these episodes.

00:08:39.541 --> 00:08:41.000
Thank you for coming on this journey
with us.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.500
I really am grateful.

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.166
It's a very enriching part of my life
to be a part of this channel

00:08:46.166 --> 00:08:50.166
and also to have a deeper relationship
with that part of our world

00:08:50.166 --> 00:08:52.916
that is so invisible to us
but is always out there.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.208
And I hope very much that we can continue
inviting people

00:08:57.208 --> 00:09:01.000
into that world through our videos,
but also through this microscope.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.916
And if you capture something
that you would like to share with us,

00:09:04.916 --> 00:09:07.041
we again
have that form on microcosmos.store.

00:09:07.041 --> 00:09:08.416
From all of us here

00:09:08.416 --> 00:09:11.541
at Journey to the Microcosmos,
including Matt sitting right there

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:13.375
filming me in my office.

00:09:13.375 --> 00:09:18.250
Thank you so much for your support
and your interest in this bizarre world

00:09:18.375 --> 00:09:19.833
that we all get to inhabit.

